,0],["syntax/templates","syntax/language",27,1],["syntax/templates","syntax/recipes",1,0]],"links":{"uses":[["model","The elliptic model of integer indices",null,3],["compiler","Exact precision computation and polynomial compilation",null,2],["main","The main theorem and its hypotheses",null,1]],"uses_total":3,"used_by":[["size","The cutoff and polynomial size",null,21],["multiplication","Multiplication and the finite false-triple guard",null,18],["compiler","Exact precision computation and polynomial compilation",null,13],["assembly","Assembly and the ordinary-value relation",null,13],["model","The elliptic model of integer indices",null,7],["route-ap","Route AP: progressions of good primes in place of the prime pattern",null,3]],"used_by_total":6},"results":[{"id":"syntax/template-semantics","title":"Semantics of templates and recipes","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"ZInQ.Syntax.Template.denote, ZInQ.Syntax.Recipe.denote, ZInQ.Syntax.ExPoly.Rel","summary":"Polynomials live in $\\mathbb Z[X_i : i\\in\\mathbb N]$ and every variable ranges over $\\mathbb Q$. A rational assignment is a map $x:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb Q$, and $f(x)$ is the value of $f$ at it.","route":"../roadmap/syntax/index.html#template-semantics"},{"id":"syntax/sum-of-squares-semantics","title":"Sums of squares and products over the rationals","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.Syntax.Formula.ev_compile_eq_zero_iff, ZInQ.Syntax.ev_sum_sq_eq_zero_iff, ZInQ.Syntax.ev_prod_eq_zero_iff","summary":"For every formula $\\varphi$ and every $x:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb Q$, In particular, for polynomials $f_1,\\dots,f_k$, $\\sum_i f_i(x)^2=0\\iff f_1(x)=\\dots=f_k(x)=0$ and $\\prod_i f_i(x)=0\\iff f_i(x)=0$ for some $i$.","route":"../roadmap/syntax/index.html#sum-of-squares-semantics"},{"id":"syntax/nonzero-gadgets","title":"Inverse witnesses","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.Syntax.exists_mul_eq_one_iff, ZInQ.Syntax.exists_mul_pairNorm_eq_one_iff","summary":"Inverses are expressed by equations $uv=1$, never by partial division.","route":"../roadmap/syntax/index.html#nonzero-gadgets"},{"id":"syntax/field-pair-arithmetic","title":"Pair arithmetic represents the quadratic field","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.Syntax.Pair.val, ZInQ.Syntax.holds_feq, ZInQ.Syntax.holds_curve","summary":"For a rational pair $u=(u_0,u_1)$ write $[u]=u_0+u_1s\\in K_0$. For rational pairs $u,u'$ and an integer $k$:","route":"../roadmap/syntax/index.html#field-pair-arithmetic"},{"id":"syntax/division-polynomials","title":"Normalized division polynomials and their components","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"ZInQ.Syntax.normDiv, ZInQ.Syntax.divPsi, ZInQ.Syntax.psiComponent","summary":"Let $R$ be a commutative ring and $A\\in R$. The normalized division polynomials $f_n\\in R[x]$, $n\\ge0$, of $y^2=x^3+Ax$ are defined by and, for larger indices, where $L=f_{m+2}f_m^3$, $R_m=f_{m-1}f_{m+1}^3$ and $D_0=16(x^3+Ax)^2$. The recursion terminates because every index on the right is smaller than the index on the left for $n\\ge5$. The division polynomials $\\psi_n\\in R[x,y]$, $n\\in\\mathbb Z$, are","route":"../roadmap/syntax/index.html#division-polynomials"},{"id":"syntax/division-polynomials-classical","title":"Agreement with the classical division polynomials","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.Syntax.normDiv_eq_preΨ', ZInQ.Syntax.divPsi_eq_Ψ, ZInQ.Syntax.mk_divPsi, ZInQ.Syntax.psiEval_eq_evalEval_ψ","summary":"Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $A\\in R$, and let $W_A$ be the Weierstrass curve $y^2=x^3+Ax$ ($a_1=a_2=a_3=a_6=0$, $a_4=A$), so that $b_2=b_6=0$, $b_4=2A$, $b_8=-A^2$. Let $\\mathrm{pre}\\Psi_n\\in R[x]$ and $\\Psi_n\\in R[x,y]$ ($n\\in\\mathbb Z$) be the classical normalized division polynomials of $W_A$: $\\mathrm{pre}\\Psi_n$ is the normalized elliptic divisibility sequence with initial values…","route":"../roadmap/syntax/index.html#division-polynomials-classical"},{"id":"syntax/division-polynomial-multiplication","title":"The multiplication-by-n formulas","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"ZInQ.DivisionPolynomialMultiplication","summary":"This article states, as a proposition, the classical formulas for multiplication by $n$ on $E:y^2=x^3-d^2x$ over $K_0$ (so $A=-d^2=-11243+7950s$). The proposition asserts: for every affine point $P=(x,y)\\in E(K_0)$ and every integer $n\\ne0$,","route":"../roadmap/syntax/index.html#division-polynomial-multiplication"},{"id":"syntax/fixed-scalar-semantics","title":"Semantics of the fixed-scalar templates","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"ZInQ.Syntax.scalarTemplate_rel_iff_pointCode, ZInQ.Syntax.scalarTemplate_rel_iff_iota","summary":"Let $e$ be a fixed integer expression with $n=\\mathrm{val}(e)\\ge1$, let $p$ be the code of a point $P\\in2E(K_0)$ and $r\\in\\mathbb Q^5$. Then In particular, on codes of $2E(K_0)=\\mathbb ZP_0$, $\\mathrm{Rel}_{S_e}(\\iota(a),r)\\iff r=\\iota(na)$.","route":"../roadmap/syntax/index.html#fixed-scalar-semantics"},{"id":"syntax/template-member","title":"The membership template","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"ZInQ.Syntax.memberTemplate, ZInQ.Syntax.memberTemplate_rel_iff","summary":"The template $\\mathtt{member}$ has $5$ parameters and $7$ witnesses ($12$ variables). Its variables are: the point $p=(X_0,\\dots,X_4)$; the pairs $X=(X_5,X_6)$, $Y=(X_7,X_8)$ and $H=(X_9,X_{10})$; and the inverse witness $u=X_{11}$ of $\\mathrm{NZ}_K(Y)$. With $x_P=H^2-2X$ and $y_P=H\\cdot(X-x_P)-Y$, its formula is N(Y)\\,u\\doteq1,\\ (2Y)\\cdot H\\doteq3X^2+A,\\ x(p)\\doteq x_P,\\ y(p)\\doteq y_P\\bigr)\\Bigr),$$ and, having…","route":"../roadmap/syntax/index.html#template-member"},{"id":"syntax/template-raw-add","title":"The raw addition template","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"ZInQ.Syntax.rawAddTemplate, ZInQ.Syntax.rawAddTemplate_rel_iff","summary":"The template $\\mathtt{raw\\_add}$ has